new('DECLARATION','''<p>We hereby declare that the project report entitled <b>“EV Charging Station Management System”</b>, submitted for the subject <b>OOPS USING C++</b> at Delhi Technical Campus, Greater Noida, describes the work undertaken by our team under the guidance of <b>Ms. Kajal Kumari</b>.</p><p>The report explains the object-oriented design, charging algorithms, implementation, customer interface and testing of the project. The implementation descriptions are based on the accompanying source files. External libraries, frameworks and project resources used in the system are identified in the report.</p><p>The system is an educational simulation. It demonstrates charging decisions and software workflows without operating physical charging equipment or collecting real payments. Features prepared for future integration are distinguished from the currently implemented local functionality.</p><p>We acknowledge the contributions of all team members and the use of open-source software in the development and presentation of this project.</p><br>'''+table(['Student Name','Enrollment Number','Signature'],[(r[1],r[2],'________________') for r in team])+'''<p>Date: ____________________</p><p>Place: Greater Noida</p>''')
new('ACKNOWLEDGEMENT','''<p>We express our sincere gratitude to <b>Ms. Kajal Kumari</b> for her guidance and support during the development of our OOPS USING C++ project, “EV Charging Station Management System”. Her guidance helped us relate classroom concepts to the design of a modular software application.</p><p>We thank the Department of Computer Science and Engineering (Artificial Intelligence &amp; Machine Learning), Delhi Technical Campus, Greater Noida, for providing the academic environment and resources required to study and implement the project.</p><p>The project provided an opportunity to apply classes, encapsulation, constructors, destructors, function overloading, templates and exception handling to a practical management problem. It also helped us understand how a C++ domain model can be connected to a browser interface, persistent storage and simulated external events.</p><p>We acknowledge the developers and maintainers of the open-source tools used in the project, including the C++ toolchain, Crow, Asio, SQLite, Python, Flutter and the mapping libraries and datasets.</p><p>Finally, we thank our teammates for their cooperation in discussing the requirements, developing the application, checking its behavior and preparing this report.</p><br><p>Ronit Sharma &nbsp;|&nbsp; Bhumika &nbsp;|&nbsp; Sarthak Kumar &nbsp;|&nbsp; Aditi Raj</p>''')
new('ABSTRACT','''<p>The growing use of electric vehicles creates a need for software that can coordinate charging requests, charger compatibility, available time, energy cost and renewable power preferences. The EV Charging Station Management System explores these decisions through a modular C++17 simulation and a supporting full-stack demonstration.</p><p>The core models vehicles, users, chargers, sessions and charging stations using encapsulated classes. A range-safety service estimates whether a vehicle can reach a station with a safety buffer. A time-bound scheduler selects a compatible charger and calculates the power needed to meet a requested deadline. A pricing engine demonstrates function overloading through flat-rate, time-of-use and solar-discount calculations. Queue components prioritize requests using emergency status, battery level, bookings, compatibility, service duration and waiting time.</p><p>The system also demonstrates solar/grid source selection and an emergency override that pauses an eligible non-emergency session and calculates compensation. Generic programming is represented by a policy-based priority queue and an event-logging function template. Invalid operations are handled through exceptions and structured API errors.</p><p>The Pluggo customer web application connects to a Crow API with SQLite persistence for visitor preferences and charging-session records. A Python WebSocket simulator supplies charging updates. Legacy operator routes and the standalone Flutter demonstration remain separate components. PostgreSQL and Keycloak resources are integration scaffolding rather than the active customer database and login system.</p><p>This report presents requirements, architecture, OOP concepts, algorithms, complexity analysis, worked calculations, implementation details, screenshots and verification. The project demonstrates software coordination; it does not control physical chargers or process real payments.</p><h2>Keywords</h2><p>C++17, object-oriented programming, EV charging, priority queue, templates, scheduling, SQLite, Crow, charging simulation.</p>''')
new('TABLE OF CONTENTS',table(['Section','Page'],[
('Declaration','2'),('Acknowledgement','3'),('Abstract','4'),('1. Introduction','6'),('2. Problem Statement and Objectives','7'),('3. Background and OOP Concepts','8'),('4. Methodology and System Architecture','9'),('5. Tools, Technologies and Data Storage','10'),('6. Algorithms and Charging Rules','11'),('7. Complexity Analysis and Worked Example','12'),('8. Implementation Guide','13'),('8. Implementation: Interface Demonstration','14'),('9. Testing and Results','15'),('10. Applications, Limitations and Future Scope','16'),('11. Conclusion','16'),('12. References','17'),('Appendix A: Demonstration Evidence','17'),('Appendix B: Code Extracts and Pseudocode','18')])+'''<p class="small">Figures: 1. System architecture (page 9); 2. Pluggo customer home (page 14); 3. Charging session interface (page 14); 4. Session history (page 17).</p>''')
new('1. INTRODUCTION','''<h2>1.1 Background</h2><p>Electric vehicles depend on charging infrastructure with different connectors, power ratings and occupancy conditions. A driver may need to reach a station safely, charge before departure and remain within a fixed budget. A station operator must coordinate competing requests while considering emergency needs and renewable energy availability.</p><p>These concerns provide a practical setting for object-oriented programming. A vehicle owns battery and connector information; a charger owns power and availability information; a session records a charging interaction. Separate services can apply scheduling and pricing rules without combining every responsibility into one large function.</p><h2>1.2 Project Overview</h2><p>The project begins with a C++17 domain engine and an interactive console demonstration. A Crow server exposes JSON APIs for the legacy operator demonstration and a separate set of customer routes. Pluggo is the customer browser application, with station discovery, vehicle preferences, simulated charging, session history and zero-money receipts.</p><h2>1.3 Motivation</h2><ul><li>Connect OOP concepts to a recognizable management problem.</li><li>Make charging constraints and queue decisions visible through examples.</li><li>Separate domain rules from interfaces and storage responsibilities.</li><li>Demonstrate validation and repeatable testing rather than only successful inputs.</li></ul><h2>1.4 Scope</h2><p>The scope includes software simulation, compatible-charger selection, deadline planning, budget calculations, priority ordering, solar/grid decisions and emergency preemption. The connected customer application adds local persistence and simulated meter events. Live utility tariffs, authenticated production accounts, real payment settlement and physical EVSE integration are outside the implemented demonstration.</p>''')
new('2. PROBLEM STATEMENT AND OBJECTIVES','''<h2>2.1 Problem Statement</h2><p>Design a modular system that represents EV charging entities and processes charging requests under connector, time, budget and power constraints. The system should explain why a request is accepted, limited or unable to meet its requested deadline, while maintaining understandable state and repeatable behavior.</p><h2>2.2 Objectives</h2><ul><li>Model domain entities using encapsulated C++ classes.</li><li>Demonstrate construction, copying, destruction, overloading and templates.</li><li>Prioritize requests and preserve deterministic ordering for ties.</li><li>Support range evaluation, pricing, source selection and emergency handling.</li><li>Present customer workflows with persistent simulated session records.</li></ul><h2>2.3 Functional Requirements</h2>'''+table(['ID','Requirement','Expected behavior'],[
('FR-01','Range and deadline','Evaluate buffered reachability and estimate a compatible charging plan.'),('FR-02','Budget and pricing','Calculate tariffs and limit energy to the remaining budget.'),('FR-03','Queue management','Rank requests; reject empty-queue removal.'),('FR-04','Energy and emergency','Choose a simulated source; preempt only an eligible normal session.'),('FR-05','Customer workflow','Discover, verify, start, stop and settle a simulated session.'),('FR-06','Persistence','Restore visitor preferences and session history from SQLite.')])+'''<h2>2.4 Non-functional Requirements</h2><p><b>Correctness:</b> respect connector and vehicle power limits. <b>Clarity:</b> keep classes and service responsibilities distinct. <b>Robustness:</b> reject invalid inputs and duplicate conflicting requests. <b>Maintainability:</b> keep the C++ domain usable independently of the browser interface.</p>''')
new('3. BACKGROUND AND OOP CONCEPTS','''<h2>3.1 Classes, Objects and Encapsulation</h2><p>Vehicle, User, Charger, Session and ChargingStation describe the main entities. Private data members prevent callers from directly changing internal values. Accessors expose relevant information, while operations such as Vehicle::setSocPercent validate changes. Session contains User and Vehicle objects; ChargingStation contains a collection of Charger objects. These are examples of composition.</p><h2>3.2 Constructors, Copying and Destructors</h2><p>The entity classes declare default, parameterized and copy constructors. Default constructors delegate to valid initial values. Parameterized constructors establish the object state and reject invalid data. Copy constructors and destructors are explicitly declared; many definitions use <b>= default</b>, relying on the normal value semantics of strings and containers. The design does not require manual memory allocation to demonstrate object lifetime.</p><h2>3.3 Overloading and Generic Programming</h2><p>PricingEngine::calculateCost has overloads with two, three and four double parameters. They represent flat pricing, solar-discount pricing and time-of-use pricing. Overload selection happens at compile time. SmartPriorityQueue&lt;T, ScorePolicy&gt; separates queue storage from its scoring policy; logEvent&lt;T&gt; accepts different printable value types.</p>'''+table(['Concept','Evidence in the implementation'],[('Abstraction','RangeSafetyService and TimeBoundScheduler expose focused operations.'),('Composition','Session contains User and Vehicle; station contains chargers.'),('Compile-time polymorphism','Function overloading and class/function templates.'),('Exception handling','invalid_argument for invalid values; underflow_error for empty queues.')])+'''<h2>3.4 Design Boundary</h2><p>The inspected core does not use an inheritance hierarchy or virtual methods to model charger types. Connector and charger categories are represented using enum classes. Therefore, runtime polymorphism and inheritance are not claimed as implemented features.</p>''')
p=new('4. METHODOLOGY AND SYSTEM ARCHITECTURE','''<h2>4.1 Development Methodology</h2><p>Identify entities and constraints, implement independent domain services, exercise them through the console, expose API operations, connect the customer interface, persist customer state and verify normal and boundary cases. This sequence keeps the OOP engine understandable before introducing web integration.</p><h2>4.2 Logical Architecture</h2>''')
