box(p,(72,536,540,726),'''<h2>4.3 Data and Event Flow</h2><p>The browser restores a visitor snapshot, verifies a connector and requests a session. The API validates the request and persists it in SQLite. Simulated meter events update energy within target and budget limits. Stop and checkout create an ended session and a stable demo receipt.</p><h2>4.4 Component Boundaries</h2><p>Customer storage uses SQLite; legacy operator sessions remain in memory. Flutter is a separate demo. PostgreSQL and Keycloak files provide scaffolding for future integration.</p>''')
new('5. TOOLS, TECHNOLOGIES AND DATA STORAGE','''<h2>5.1 Technology Selection</h2>'''+table(['Technology','Role'],[('C++17; standard library','Domain classes, algorithms, containers and exceptions.'),('CMake; CTest; clang++','Build configuration and automated feature checks.'),('Crow + standalone Asio','HTTP/JSON API and WebSocket integration.'),('HTML, CSS and JavaScript','Pluggo browser experience and operator interface.'),('SQLite','Implemented local customer persistence.'),('Python simulator','OCPP-shaped charging events over WebSocket.'),('Leaflet / OpenStreetMap','Map display and public station directory snapshot.'),('Flutter; Docker; OpenAPI','Separate mobile demo, deployment packaging and API contracts.')])+'''<h2>5.2 Implemented Data Model</h2><p>The customer_visitors table stores a visitor ID, selected vehicle and saved-station JSON. The customer_sessions table stores session ID, owner, idempotency key, request, connector, timestamps, settlement flag and a JSON payload containing session details. A unique constraint on (owner, idem) supports safe request retries.</p><p>The customer request wrapper serializes operations with a mutex and uses SQLite transactions. Owner checks keep visitor sessions separate. This is local visitor identity through a cookie, rather than a recovered account with cross-device login.</p><h2>5.3 Feasibility and Deployment</h2><p>The console needs a C++17 compiler. The connected local application additionally needs SQLite development support, Git, Python and web dependencies. No dedicated charging hardware is required. Static hosting serves the interface but cannot execute the C++ API; the connected experience requires a backend host and simulator. PostgreSQL, Keycloak and container configuration are supporting resources with separate integration and validation needs.</p>''')
new('6. ALGORITHMS AND CHARGING RULES','''<h2>6.1 Buffered Range Evaluation</h2><p>Usable energy = battery capacity × current SoC / 100. Available range = usable energy / consumption per kilometre. Required range = station distance × (1 + buffer / 100). The station is considered reachable when available range is at least the required buffered distance.</p><h2>6.2 Deadline-Based Scheduling</h2><p>Energy needed = capacity × (target SoC − current SoC) / 100. Required input power = energy needed / (available hours × charging efficiency). The scheduler scans available compatible chargers and limits each to the smaller of charger power and vehicle acceptance power. It selects the smallest sufficient capacity and throttles assigned power to the requirement. If no charger meets the deadline, it returns the fastest compatible option with deadlineFeasible set to false.</p><h2>6.3 Priority Scoring</h2>'''+table(['Condition','Score contribution'],[('Emergency request or vehicle','+1000'),('SoC below 10%; otherwise below 20%','+300; otherwise +150'),('Pre-booked request','+100'),('Compatible charger available / unavailable','+40 / −200'),('Shorter estimated service','max(0, 60 − minutes) × 0.25'),('Waiting time','min(120, non-negative whole minutes) × 0.5')])+'''<p>The template queue computes the score at insertion and breaks exact ties by insertion sequence. QueueManager instead recalculates scores when ordering its vector, using arrival time to break ties. The two components demonstrate different approaches to changing priorities.</p><h2>6.4 Solar and Emergency Decisions</h2><p>Full live solar supply receives a 15% illustrative discount; partial solar or stored energy selects grid fallback with 7.5%. Emergency override scans charging non-emergency sessions and their chargers, selecting the highest-rated compatible candidate, pausing it and returning an INR 50 compensation credit. A non-emergency request or missing eligible candidate is rejected.</p>''')
new('7. COMPLEXITY ANALYSIS AND WORKED EXAMPLE','''<h2>7.1 Complexity Summary</h2><p>Let C be the charger count, N the queued request count and S the active-session count. Scoring one request uses a fixed number of arithmetic operations.</p>'''+table(['Operation','Time','Additional / stored space'],[('Range, pricing, solar decision','O(1)','O(1)'),('Deadline scheduler / charger lookup','O(C)','O(1) working space'),('Template queue push / pop','O(log N)','O(N) stored nodes'),('Template queue top','O(1)','O(1) working space'),('QueueManager orderedRequests','O(N log N)','O(N) copied requests'),('QueueManager serveNext','O(N log N)','O(N) ordered copy'),('Emergency candidate selection','O(S × C)','O(1) working space')])+'''<h2>7.2 Worked Charging Example</h2><p>Use the domain test vehicle: 60 kWh battery, 20% initial SoC, 0.2 kWh/km consumption, CCS2 connector and 100 kW vehicle limit. Usable energy is 12 kWh, giving 60 km range. At a 20% safety buffer, a 50 km trip requires 60 km and is accepted; 51 km requires 61.2 km and is rejected.</p><p>Charging to 80% requires 60 × (80 − 20) / 100 = <b>36 kWh</b>. With one hour available and 90% efficiency, required input power is 36 / 0.9 = <b>40 kW</b>. A compatible 60 kW charger is sufficient, so the scheduler chooses it and assigns 40 kW.</p><p>With only 0.1 hours, the requirement rises to 400 kW. The 150 kW charger is limited by the vehicle to 100 kW, giving 36 / (100 × 0.9) = <b>0.4 hours</b>. The returned plan therefore cannot meet the requested deadline.</p><h2>7.3 Pricing and Budget</h2><p>20 kWh at INR 18/kWh costs INR 360. A 15% solar discount reduces it to INR 306. For an INR 300 budget at INR 20/kWh, 15 kWh is affordable in total; if 5 kWh is already delivered, 10 kWh remains. These are deterministic model calculations, not measured charger performance.</p>''')
new('8. IMPLEMENTATION GUIDE','''<h2>8.1 Source Organization</h2>'''+table(['File / directory','Responsibility'],[('include/ev_system.hpp','Entity declarations, service interfaces and queue template.'),('src/ev_system.cpp','Validation, scheduling, pricing, queue and energy rules.'),('src/main.cpp','Interactive console and automated demonstration.'),('src/api_server.cpp','Crow server and legacy operator endpoints.'),('src/customer_api.cpp','Customer API, SQLite storage and simulator boundary.'),('frontend/; ocpp-simulator/','Browser interfaces and Python simulated events.'),('tests/ev_tests.cpp; tests/api/','Domain checks and customer integration verification.')])+'''<h2>8.2 Build and Run</h2><pre>cmake -S . -B build
cmake --build build
ctest --test-dir build --output-on-failure
./build/ev_charging</pre><p>The console offers deadline planning, budget charging, queue operations, solar simulation, emergency override and a complete automated demonstration. Platform-specific executable paths may vary.</p><pre>python3 -m pip install -r ocpp-simulator/requirements.txt
python3 tools/run-local.py --build</pre><p>The local customer launcher starts the browser proxy on port 4173, the C++ API on 18080 and the charging simulator. Open http://127.0.0.1:4173/#home. The API build requires SQLite support and Crow/Asio dependencies.</p><h2>8.3 Customer Request Lifecycle</h2><p>Bootstrap establishes a visitor cookie. Session creation requires the application request header and an idempotency key. The server validates connector, vehicle, target and budget; it owns energy and pricing calculations. Stop and checkout are retry-safe. Checkout records a simulated receipt and collects INR 0. Network failures show a retry state; standalone browser simulation is selected explicitly using ?demo=1.</p>''')
