new('9. TESTING AND RESULTS','''<h2>9.1 Verification Approach</h2><p>The C++ feature executable was compiled from the inspected source using clang++ with C++17 and warning flags. It completed successfully and printed: <b>“All EV feature checks passed.”</b> The checks use deterministic inputs and compare calculated values or expected state transitions.</p>'''+table(['Test group','Expected / verified domain behavior'],[('Range boundary','50 km accepted; 51 km rejected with the test safety buffer.'),('Deadline and compatibility','40 kW plan for one hour; impossible deadline flagged; incompatible connector rejected.'),('Pricing and budget','Flat 360; time-of-use 290; solar 306; remaining allowance 10 kWh.'),('Priority and ties','Emergency before booked before ordinary; FIFO for equal template scores.'),('Empty queue','Removal throws underflow_error.'),('Solar selection','Full solar, partial-solar fallback and night grid cases distinguished.'),('Emergency override','Normal session paused; charger transferred; credit returned; invalid preemption rejected.')])+'''<h2>9.2 Customer Integration Verification</h2><p>The repository contains a real-process C++/SQLite API suite covering restart persistence, visitor isolation, origin/request-header checks, server validation, idempotency, connector contention, target caps, receipt replay and authenticated WebSocket simulator metering. The verification status for this report is recorded below.</p><p><b>INTEGRATION_STATUS</b></p><h2>9.3 Interpretation</h2><p>Passing checks support the tested local software behaviors. They do not demonstrate production throughput, complete OCPP conformance, physical charging safety, real vehicle telemetry or payment processing. The report includes no invented latency, memory benchmark or charging-efficiency measurement.</p>''')
new('10. APPLICATIONS, LIMITATIONS AND FUTURE SCOPE','''<h2>10.1 Applications</h2><ul><li>Classroom demonstrations of C++ OOP and generic programming.</li><li>Exploration of deadline, budget and compatibility constraints.</li><li>Comparison of static heap scores and recalculated request ordering.</li><li>Prototype customer journeys for station discovery and simulated charging.</li></ul><h2>10.2 Current Limitations</h2><p>The charging model uses simplified constant-power calculations and does not reproduce battery tapering, thermal behavior or grid control. The solar manager chooses a source and discount; it is not a complete interval-by-interval storage dispatch model. Emergency handling changes session state and returns a credit; automated resumption, financial settlement and a persisted fairness ledger require additional implementation.</p><p>Customer identity is visitor-cookie based. Legacy operator sessions remain in memory. The public station map is a directory snapshot rather than a live network inventory. The Flutter demonstration and legacy routes are separate from the persistent customer browser workflow. Physical hardware, production identity and real payments are outside the implemented scope.</p><h2>10.3 Future Scope</h2><ul><li>Integrate production accounts, JWT validation and recovery.</li><li>Evaluate PostgreSQL migration and multi-instance coordination.</li><li>Add audited charger connectivity and richer charging curves.</li><li>Persist emergency compensation, resumption and queue events.</li><li>Measure performance with repeatable, documented workloads.</li></ul><h1>11. CONCLUSION</h1><p>The project connects encapsulated C++ entities, overloaded pricing functions, policy-based templates and exception handling to an EV charging management problem. Its domain rules are demonstrated through a console and supporting APIs, while Pluggo adds a persistent customer simulation. The result is an inspectable educational platform whose current behavior and future integrations can be explained separately.</p>''')
p=new('12. REFERENCES','''<p class="small">Implementation sources inspected for this report are from the accompanying project checkout. Paths below identify the relevant evidence; they do not imply external performance or hardware certification.</p><p>[1] Team project repository: <a href="https://github.com/Bhumika-gaba23/EV-charging-management">github.com/Bhumika-gaba23/EV-charging-management</a>.</p><p>[2] include/ev_system.hpp and src/ev_system.cpp — domain entities, services, queue policy and algorithms.</p><p>[3] src/main.cpp and tests/ev_tests.cpp — console workflow and domain verification.</p><p>[4] src/customer_api.cpp and docs/customer-backend.md — customer identity, SQLite persistence and simulator behavior.</p><p>[5] CMakeLists.txt and openapi-customer.yaml — build dependencies and customer API contract.</p><p>[6] tests/api/customer_integration.py and docs/ui-audit/backend-connected/ — integration checks and saved UI evidence.</p><h1>APPENDIX A: DEMONSTRATION EVIDENCE</h1><p>For a presentation, demonstrate vehicle selection, station verification, session start, simulated charging, stop, zero-money checkout and session history. Use the console separately to explain priority scoring, deadline scheduling and emergency preemption.</p>''')
